Page 9 – Scope of the Project
The project is designed for a small school library.

Included features:
- Adding new books.
- Displaying all available books.
- Searching books by title, author or category.
- Registering library members.
- Issuing books.
- Returning books.
- Displaying issued-book records.
- Automatically updating book availability.
Limitations of the scope:

This version does not include online accounts, barcode scanning, fine payment or email reminders. These features can be added in future versions.
Page 10 – Existing System
The traditional library system uses paper registers, index cards or spreadsheets.

Working of the manual system:
1. The librarian checks a register to find a book.
2. The member's name and book details are written down.
3. The issue date and return date are recorded.
4. On return, the librarian locates the entry.
5. The available number of copies is updated manually.
Disadvantages:
- Repeated writing takes time.
- Handwriting can be difficult to read.
- Records may be misplaced.
- Searching can be slow.
- Mistakes may occur while updating records.
Page 11 – Proposed System
The proposed system is a menu-driven Python application connected to an SQLite database.

The librarian selects an option from the main menu. The program calls the related function.

For example:
- The Add Book option stores new book details.
- The Search Book option searches for a particular book.
- The Issue Book option checks availability and records the transaction.
- The Return Book option updates the transaction status.
SQLite stores all information in a local file named library.db.

This makes the system more organised and reduces manual work.
